T4 CI matrix round 3 — windows-latest last red leg (gh run 42163117877,
branch b85-npm-tail). 2026-11-06.

== Item 1: TestWriteDurableFileNeverTorn "rename ... Access is denied"
   (PRODUCT, internal/mcp) ==

Failure: write A: rename .durable-*.tmp driver.lua: Access is denied +
TempDir cleanup unlinkat driver.lua: being used by another process.

Diagnosis: the brief's suspected rename-of-open-SOURCE was already
correct in the code (writeDurableFile closes the temp BEFORE
os.Rename; re-verified). The held handle is the TARGET's: the test's 4
reader goroutines loop os.ReadFile(driver.lua), and Go's opens carry
FILE_SHARE_READ|WRITE but NOT FILE_SHARE_DELETE - MoveFileEx cannot
replace a file any reader has open on windows (unix renames over open
files freely). Secondary cleanup failure = the early t.Fatalf left the
reader goroutines running into TempDir RemoveAll (same share
violation). Only one product temp+rename site exists in the repo
(grep os.CreateTemp|os.Rename|\.tmp over cmd/ internal/ hil/ minus
tests: manifest.go alone; hil matches are board-side fs ops).

Fix: replaceFile(src, dst) split -
  internal/mcp/rename_unix.go     //go:build !windows  (plain rename)
  internal/mcp/rename_windows.go  (bounded retry: ERROR_SHARING_VIOLATION
    32 / ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED 5, 10ms x 200 = ~2s worst case; readers are
    transient, a gap lands; each successful rename is still atomic -
    absent-or-complete unchanged; non-sharing errors return immediately)
writeDurableFile now calls replaceFile; doc comment updated. With
writes succeeding, no early Fatalf => readers join before cleanup =>
the cleanup failure disappears too.

== Item 2: TestResolveUSBDeviceDirectAndUnknown candidate mention
   (TEST, internal/replclient) ==

Failure: error "...tried usb-No_Such_Device-if00, \dev\serial\by-id\
usb-No_Such_Device-if00" does not mention candidate "/dev/serial/by-id".
Diagnosis: filepath.Join Cleans separators per-OS - on windows
"/dev/serial/by-id" renders "\dev\serial\by-id". The product prints the
joined (OS-native) form; asserting a literal unix spelling was the
test bug. Chosen fix (smaller/safer, per brief): the test now asserts
filepath.Join(byIDDir, "usb-No_Such_Device-if00") - pinned to the exact
construction under test, portable; product user-facing text unchanged.

== Item 3: TestBindSockConcurrentRaceGracefulLosers round 7
   (PRODUCT, internal/server) ==

Failure: winners=2 bare=1, non-graceful loser "remove stale sock ...
cannot find the file specified" - the exact TOCTOU
sockclaim_windows.go called "residual, accepted (theoretical)": racer
B's bindDirect got WSAEADDRINUSE, re-probed inside winner A's
bound-but-not-yet-listening window, dial refused -> judged stale ->
removed (another racer beat it to the remove: ENOENT bare loser; the
remover rebound: second winner). CI disproved "theoretical".

Fix: REAL transition mutex, per the brief's protocol. bindSock now
delegates to a platform seam bindSockPlatform (mkdir stays shared):
  sockclaim_unix.go: prelude (stat/probe/remove, verbatim) + bindClaim
    - unix behavior byte-identical, link protocol untouched.
  sockclaim_windows.go: rewritten -
    claim   O_CREATE|O_EXCL <sock>.lck (atomic on NTFS: exactly one
            holder);
    hold    bindLocked = stale-check prelude + bindDirect, then
            release (close handle, delete lock);
    wait    non-holder polls (2ms): sock alive -> graceful
            ErrAlreadyListening; lock gone -> retry claim; past
            transitionGrace (500ms, two orders beyond the ms-scale
            transition) with sock dead AND lock present -> holder
            crashed: remove stale sock + stale lock, retry (bounded 4).
    after   liveness remains the sock probe - the lock serializes the
            TRANSITION only.
  Exactly one binder at a time => one winner, all others graceful;
  crash mid-transition self-heals. Comment rewritten from "residual
  window acknowledged" to the protocol.

